      "The wild, tragic romance between Heathcliff and Catherine Earnshaw unfolded against the brooding backdrop of the Yorkshire moors. Catherine loved Heathcliff with a passion that terrified her: 'Whatever our souls are made of, his and mine are the same; and Linton's is as different as a moonbeam from lightning, or frost from fire!' Yet she married the wealthy, refined Edgar Linton of Thrushcross Grange to elevate her social position.",
      "Heathcliff vanished for three years, returning wealthy, cultured, but consumed by an unquenchable lust for revenge against the Earnshaws and Lintons. Catherine's heart was torn between the two men, leading to her death in childbirth. On her deathbed, Heathcliff cursed her spirit: 'Catherine Earnshaw, may you not rest as long as I am living! You said I killed you—haunt me, then! Be with me always—take any form—drive me mad! only do not leave me in this abyss, where I cannot find you! I cannot live without my life! I cannot live without my soul!'",
